BATCH={'key':'pueblo_wpln','label':'Pueblo, Colorado: downtown and the Mesa Junction','added':'2026-11-23 12:33'}
PLACES=[
N('orman-adams-house-pueblo','Orman–Adams House','Pueblo','co','landmark','standing',38.25481,-104.62813,'exact','view','drive','','1890',
  'A Richardsonian Romanesque house built in 1890 to designs by William A. Lang for James B. Orman, who lived in it from 1891 to 1918 and was governor of Colorado from 1901 to 1903. From 1918 it was the home of former governor Alva Adams and then of his son Alva B. Adams, a United States senator, who died in 1941. From 1952 it was offices of the public school district; Wikipedia says it was sold to a family in 2022. Listed on the National Register in 1976.',
  'A large stone house of the 1890s among the other big houses of South Pueblo. Seen from the street; interior not checked.',
  'Home to two Colorado governors and a senator, in heavy Romanesque stone.',
  'Privately owned (sold in 2022, per Wikipedia). View from West Orman Avenue only; street parking.',
  [W('Orman-Adams House'),L('History Colorado: Orman-Adams House','https://www.historycolorado.org/location/orman-adams-house')],brick='?'),
N('pueblo-county-courthouse','Pueblo County Courthouse','Pueblo','co','civic','standing',38.27670,-104.60945,'exact','view','drive','','1908–1912',
  'The third building to serve as Pueblo County\'s courthouse, designed by the New York architect Albert W. Ross with G. Wroe and built in 1908 to 1912. It is faced with smooth white Turkey Creek sandstone over two-foot-thick brick walls on a Pikes Peak granite base, and has an unusual double dome sheathed in copper. Listed on the National Register in 1975.',
  'Corinthian-columned fronts on a landscaped full city block between 10th and 11th streets. Inside, per SAH Archipedia and Wikipedia: onyx and marble floors, bronze fittings, and stencil murals and portraits by the Pueblo painter J. Charles Schnorr, some with gold leaf, in the rotunda, the commissioners\' chamber and the courtrooms. Opening hours for visitors not checked.',
  'A copper double dome and gold-leaf murals by a local painter.',
  'Pueblo County building at 215 West 10th Street, in the middle of downtown. The grounds are open as a public square; the inside is a working county building and its hours were not checked. Street and metered parking around the block.',
  [W('Pueblo County Courthouse'),L('SAH Archipedia: Pueblo County Courthouse',ARCH+'9363')],brick='no'),
N('pueblo-federal-building','Pueblo Federal Building (old post office)','Pueblo','co','civic','standing',38.27132,-104.60880,'exact','view','drive','','1897',
  'Built in 1897 to designs by William Aiken, Supervising Architect of the Treasury, at a cost of $275,000, as Pueblo\'s federal courthouse and post office. SAH Archipedia calls it a fine specimen of Beaux-Arts Renaissance Revival: an Italian palazzo of gray brick sheathed in Bedford, Indiana, limestone. Listed on the National Register in 1978.',
  'Look for the bronze window guards on the main floor, each with an embossed U.S. seal, and eagles and scales of justice carved in the stone trim. What it is used for today and whether it can be entered were not checked.',
  'An 1897 limestone palazzo at the main corner downtown.',
  'At 421 North Main Street, southwest corner of 5th Street. Owner and present use not checked: view from the street. Street and metered parking.',
  [W('Pueblo Federal Building'),L('SAH Archipedia: Old Federal Building',ARCH+'9358')],brick='no'),
N('union-depot-pueblo','Pueblo Union Depot','Pueblo','co','rail','standing',38.26293,-104.61722,'exact','view','drive','stations','1889–1890',
  'A Richardsonian Romanesque station built in 1889 to 1890 to designs by the Chicago architect Frank V. Newall, shared at first by the Denver & Rio Grande, the Santa Fe, the Colorado & Southern, the Missouri Pacific and the Rock Island. Theodore Roosevelt and Woodrow Wilson arrived here. Passenger trains no longer call; Union Pacific and BNSF still use the tracks. Restored in 1992 and listed on the National Register in 1975.',
  'A castle of rough red Manitou sandstone with a six-story clock tower over the B Street entrance. Inside, per SAH Archipedia: cast-iron columns painted to look like wood, Art Nouveau stained-glass transoms, golden oak wainscoting and a parquet ceiling, and an Art Deco soda fountain; it calls it the least changed of Colorado\'s large stations. Wikipedia says the depot is privately owned: interior hours not checked.',
  'A red-stone castle of a station with its 1890 interior, no trains stopping.',
  'Privately owned (Wikipedia). At Victoria Avenue and B Street, at the south end of the Union Avenue historic district. View from B Street; the tracks beside it are active, so keep off them. Street parking.',
  [W('Union Depot (Pueblo, Colorado)'),L('SAH Archipedia: Union Depot',ARCH+'9395')],brick='no'),
N('old-central-high-pueblo','Old Central High School (Stone Schoolhouse)','Pueblo','co','civic','standing',38.25292,-104.62060,'exact','view','drive','','1882',
  'Pueblo\'s Central High School opened here in 1882 in a two-story Italianate building designed by C. R. Manning, with Weston & Trost as local supervising architects. Superintendent Preston Search began here the "Pueblo Plan", an early scheme letting pupils advance at their own pace. The high school moved to a new building on Orman Avenue in 1905 and this one became an elementary school. It was listed on the National Register in 1979, was due to be pulled down that year, and was saved in 1980 to house the Pueblo Ballet.',
  'Rough-faced light pink rhyolite with smooth cream Manitou sandstone trim, and an 80-foot bell tower with a mansard roof over the entrance. Present use not checked; seen from the street. The present Central High School (1906, brick, 216 East Orman Avenue) is a different building.',
  'Pink volcanic stone and an 80-foot bell tower, saved from demolition in 1980.',
  'At 431 East Pitkin Avenue at Madison Street. Owner and present use not checked: view from the street. Street parking.',
  [L('SAH Archipedia: Old Central High School',ARCH+'9380'),L('Trost Society: Central High School','https://trostsociety.org/buildings/central-high-school/'),L('Wetmore Historical Society: Pueblo Central High School','https://wetmorehistoricalsociety.com/2026/04/21/pueblo-central-high-school/'),W('Central High School (Pueblo, Colorado)')],brick='no'),
N('vail-hotel-pueblo','Vail Hotel','Pueblo','co','hotel','standing',38.26642,-104.61172,'exact','view','drive','hotels','1910–1911',
  'Named for the Pueblo newspaperman John E. Vail and designed by J. M. Gile with the Denver architects Willison and Fallis, it was advertised as the most modern hotel west of Chicago. Wikipedia gives 1910 for its building and SAH Archipedia September 11, 1911 for its dedication. After decades of disuse the Pueblo Housing Authority bought it in 1979 and restored it, and since 1984 it has been housing for elderly and disabled residents. Listed on the National Register in 1978.',
  'Five stories of white brick in Renaissance Revival dress, with an Ionic-columned porch and storefront transoms of stained glass bearing a V. Archipedia says the lobby keeps great columns of red Beulah marble with carved wooden scrolls, and that the Pueblo County Historical Society had its museum and library in the basement; whether that is still so was not checked. It is a home: view from the street.',
  'A 1911 grand hotel that became public housing and kept its marble lobby.',
  'Owned by the Housing Authority of the City of Pueblo, at 217 South Grand Avenue, corner of Union Avenue. Residents\' building: look from the street. Street parking.',
  [W('Vail Hotel'),L('SAH Archipedia: Hotel Vail',ARCH+'9393')],brick='no'),
N('mechanics-building-pueblo','Mechanics Building (Masonic Building)','Pueblo','co','civic','standing',38.26943,-104.60876,'exact','view','drive','','1890–1891',
  'A five-story office block built in 1890 to 1891 to designs by Francis W. Cooper, with the Mechanics Savings Bank as its main tenant. A Masonic lodge bought it in 1910 and made part of it a meeting hall. The Masons sold it in 1946 to the American Furniture Company, its main tenant since. Listed on the National Register in 1983.',
  'Pressed brick and red sandstone, a two-story bay window framed in copper at the center, a row of arched windows, decorative brickwork on the fifth story and a carved stone fan in the cornice (SAH Archipedia). Interior not checked.',
  'An 1891 bank block turned Masonic hall, with a copper bay window.',
  'At 207–211 North Main Street. Privately owned; whether the ground floor is an open shop was not checked: view from the street. Street parking.',
  [W('Mechanics Building'),L('SAH Archipedia: Mechanics and Masonic Hall',ARCH+'9355')],brick='?'),
N('tutt-building-pueblo','Tutt Building','Pueblo','co','civic','standing',38.268611,-104.608056,'site','view','drive','','1890',
  'A triangular commercial building of 1890, built for the businessman Charles Leaming Tutt Sr., who owned it until he died in 1911. Adolph Coors bought it that year and passed it to the Coors Brewing Company in 1913; from 1947 to 1982 it housed a jewelry company. Wikipedia calls it one of the few intact commercial buildings downtown. Listed on the National Register in 1983, and it has a historical marker.',
  'A flatiron-shaped corner building whose walls are a foot of brick with a red face. Interior not checked.',
  'A little 1890 flatiron in red brick, once owned by Adolph Coors.',
  'At 421 Central Plaza (History Colorado). Privately owned: view from the street. Street parking.',
  [W('Tutt Building'),L('History Colorado: Tutt Building','https://www.historycolorado.org/location/tutt-building')],brick='yes'),
N('thatcher-building-pueblo','Thatcher Building (First National Bank)','Pueblo','co','civic','standing',38.27194,-104.60887,'exact','view','drive','','1914',
  'A seven-story headquarters built in 1914 for the Thatcher family\'s First National Bank, part of a financial empire that SAH Archipedia says took in thirty-two banks. John A. Thatcher had opened a general store in Pueblo in 1863. It was designed by the Chicago firm Schmidt, Garden and Martin. Archipedia (1997) calls it little altered and says it had been the Pueblo headquarters of Colorado National Banks since 1982; its present owner was not checked.',
  'Red Manitou sandstone on a Pikes Peak granite base: an arcaded ground floor with carved Sullivan-style panels and metal ornaments along the eaves. Archipedia says the lobby keeps murals by Holsing and Company of Chicago showing the Thatchers\' businesses, from cattle to smelting. Interior hours not checked.',
  'A 1914 red-sandstone bank tower with its painted lobby.',
  'At 501 North Main Street, northwest corner of 5th Street, across from the old post office. View from the street; whether the lobby is open to the public was not checked. Street parking.',
  [L('SAH Archipedia: First National Bank',ARCH+'9359')],brick='no'),
N('first-presbyterian-pueblo','First Presbyterian Church','Pueblo','co','worship','standing',38.27595,-104.60990,'exact','view','drive','','1890',
  'An 1890 church in red sandstone, in a style owing much to Richardson. SAH Archipedia calls it the oldest and one of the finest of the many Colorado churches founded by the Reverend Sheldon Jackson. The Thatcher family gave its Tiffany glass.',
  'Red-orange stone of many sizes laid with dark red mortar, Gothic-arched windows with Tiffany glass, and a tall shingled steeple (SAH Archipedia). Interior not checked; service times not read from a page of the church\'s own.',
  'An 1890 red-stone church with Tiffany windows given by the Thatchers.',
  'At the southeast corner of Court Street and 10th Street (220 West 10th Street), across from the courthouse. An active church: view from the street. Street parking.',
  [L('SAH Archipedia: First Presbyterian Church',ARCH+'9362')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('sacred-heart-cathedral-pueblo','Cathedral of the Sacred Heart','Pueblo','co','worship','standing',38.27690,-104.61205,'exact','open','drive','','1912–1913',
  'The Jesuit-run parish of St. Ignatius, Pueblo\'s first, began in 1873; its church burned in 1882 and was rebuilt. A new church dedicated to the Sacred Heart was begun in 1912 to designs by the Denver architects Robert Willison and Montana S. Fallis. It became a cathedral in 1942, when Pueblo became the seat of a new diocese, and was renovated in 1989. Listed on the National Register in 1989.',
  'A French Gothic Revival church of speckled blond brick with terracotta trim and a red tile roof, a corner spire, two square towers and a rose window over a triple-arched entrance. Archipedia says the stained glass is by the Emil Frei studio of St. Louis, and a glass south pavilion was added in 1989. Open at its Masses.',
  'A 1913 Gothic cathedral with Emil Frei glass and a corner spire.',
  'The cathedral of the Diocese of Pueblo, at 414 West 11th Street (corner of Grand Avenue). Mass times from the parish\'s own page, read November 2026; parish office Monday to Friday 9:30 a.m. to 2:30 p.m. Street parking.',
  [W('Cathedral of the Sacred Heart (Pueblo, Colorado)'),L('SAH Archipedia: Sacred Heart Cathedral',ARCH+'9364'),L('Cathedral of the Sacred Heart','https://www.shcathedral.net/')],brick='no',
  hours='Mass Saturday 4 p.m.; Sunday 8:30 and 11:30 a.m.; Tuesday to Friday noon'),
N('temple-emanuel-pueblo','Temple Emanuel','Pueblo','co','worship','standing',38.27964,-104.61208,'exact','view','drive','','1900',
  'Pueblo\'s oldest synagogue, built in 1900 for a Reform congregation that began in 1898 as the Ladies Temple Association. It was designed by the Pueblo architect and builder Jacob M. Gile in the Queen Anne style with Neoclassical and Richardsonian touches, and has been little altered. A separate education building shaped like a Star of David was added in 1963. Listed on the National Register in 1996.',
  'A one-story masonry building with a front gable, two polygonal towers and an entrance under a Syrian arch. Interior not checked; service times not found on a page of the congregation\'s own.',
  'A small 1900 synagogue with twin towers, little changed.',
  'An active congregation at 1325 North Grand Avenue: view from the street. Street parking.',
  [W('Temple Emanuel (Pueblo, Colorado)')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('pueblo-city-park-zoo','Pueblo City Park Zoo','Pueblo','co','inst','standing',38.26063,-104.65551,'site','open','drive','','1933–1940',
  'The core of the Pueblo Zoo was built in 1933 to 1940 in City Park by three New Deal agencies: the Public Works Administration, the Civil Works Administration and the Works Progress Administration (History Colorado; Wikipedia adds the Civilian Conservation Corps). That core is listed on the National Register as the Pueblo City Park Zoo. The zoo, about 25 acres, is run by the Pueblo Zoological Society.',
  'The Depression-era buildings stand within a working zoo of some 500 animals; which of them are open to visitors was not checked.',
  'A zoo built by New Deal crews in the 1930s, still in use.',
  'Run by the Pueblo Zoological Society in City Park, 3455 Nuckolls Avenue. Admission charged (see its page); last paid admission an hour before closing; closed on major holidays. Hours from the zoo\'s page, read November 2026. Parking in the City Park lot beside the entrance.',
  [W('Pueblo Zoo'),L('History Colorado: Pueblo City Park Zoo','https://www.historycolorado.org/location/pueblo-city-park-zoo'),L('Pueblo Zoo: visit','https://www.pueblozoo.org/visit')],brick='?',
  th=[38.26103,-104.65507],thn='City Park lot off Nuckolls Avenue',
  hours='October to April daily 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.; May to September daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('cfi-steelworks-office-pueblo','Minnequa Steel Works office and dispensary (Steelworks Museum)','Pueblo','co','industry','standing',38.23788,-104.61244,'exact','open','drive','','1901–1902',
  'The headquarters of the Colorado Fuel and Iron Company, whose steel mill on the south side of Pueblo was the city\'s main industry for most of its history. The main office building was built in 1901 and served until the early 1990s; the six-room Spanish Mission style dispensary opened in 1902 and treated mill workers, about 75 cases a day in 1902 when the payroll passed 5,000. Listed on the National Register in 2002 and made a National Historic Landmark in 2021.',
  'The dispensary has been the Steelworks Museum of Industry and Culture since January 19, 2007, with exhibits on coal mining, steel, railroads and labor. The main office building next door is not part of the museum yet.',
  'The steel company\'s 1901 offices, its clinic now a museum.',
  'Owned and run by the Steelworks Center of the West, 215 Canal Street, by the steel mill. Admission $10 adults, $4 children (its page, read November 2026). The mill beside it is a working plant: keep to the museum. Parking not checked; a mapped lot is on Canal Street just west.',
  [L('Minnequa Steel Works Office Building and Dispensary (Wikipedia)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minnequa_Steel_Works_Office_Building_and_Dispensary,_Colorado_Fuel_and_Iron_Company'),W('Colorado Fuel and Iron'),L('Steelworks Center of the West','https://www.steelworks.us/')],brick='?',
  hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 3 p.m.; closed Sunday'),
N('pueblo-city-hall-memorial-hall','Pueblo City Hall and Memorial Hall','Pueblo','co','civic','standing',38.26567,-104.61117,'exact','view','drive','','1917–1919',
  'City Hall, built after Pueblo, Central Pueblo and South Pueblo joined into one city in 1886, was designed by W. E. Stickney and finished in 1917; the Union Avenue district article says the terms of that joining required City Hall to stand in this district. Memorial Hall, an auditorium added in 1919 to New York designs (SAH Archipedia credits "Godey and Haskins"), echoes its cornice but is far more ornate. Archipedia says Woodrow Wilson made his last public address here.',
  'City Hall is brick trimmed with terracotta; Memorial Hall is sheathed entirely in cream terracotta, with a slender cupola and Ionic columns. Interior not checked.',
  'A city hall and a cream terracotta auditorium where Woodrow Wilson made his last public speech.',
  'City of Pueblo buildings at Union Avenue and City Hall Place (1 City Hall Place). Memorial Hall is used for events; when it can be seen inside was not checked. Street parking.',
  [L('SAH Archipedia: Pueblo City Hall',ARCH+'9394'),W('Union Avenue Historic Commercial District')],brick='?'),
N('columbus-monument-pueblo','Christopher Columbus Monument','Pueblo','co','curio','standing',38.259444,-104.620833,'area','public','drive','','1905',
  'A monument to Christopher Columbus put up in 1905 by Pueblo\'s Italian community. It is listed on the National Register (reference 11000608). It has drawn protests and calls for its removal; KOAA reported the city\'s decision to keep it, at least for then.',
  'A statue on a pedestal in the Mesa Junction neighborhood. Not checked on the ground.',
  'A 1905 statue raised by Pueblo\'s Italian immigrants, still argued over.',
  'On public ground in the Mesa Junction (the Abriendo Avenue and Union Avenue area); look from the sidewalk. Street parking. The pin is the Register list point and may be off the statue.',
  [L('KOAA: Pueblo to keep Columbus statue, at least for now','https://www.koaa.com/news/covering-colorado/pueblo-to-keep-columbus-statue-at-least-for-now'),L('National Register listings in Pueblo County (Wikipedia)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Register_of_Historic_Places_listings_in_Pueblo_County,_Colorado')],brick='?',conf='mixed'),
]
